    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the math.\n\nThe formula for calculating monthly interest on a student loan is:\n\nMonthly Interest = (Outstanding Principal Balance x Monthly Interest Rate) / 12\n\nWhere:\n\n* Outstanding Principal Balance is the current balance of the loan\n* Monthly Interest Rate is the annual interest rate divided by 12 (e.g., 6% annual interest rate becomes 0.5% monthly interest rate)\n\nFor example, let's say you have a $10,000 loan with an annual interest rate of 6%. The monthly interest rate would be 0.5% (6%/12). If you have a $10,000 outstanding balance, the monthly interest would be:\n\nMonthly Interest = ($10,000 x 0.005) / 12 = $41.67\n\nNow, let's say you make a monthly payment of $500. The payment is typically divided into two parts: interest and principal.\n\nThe interest portion of the payment is calculated by multiplying the outstanding balance by the monthly interest rate:\n\nInterest Portion = Outstanding Principal Balance x Monthly Interest Rate\n= $10,000 x 0.005\n= $50\n\nThe principal portion",
