      "content": "You're right to be concerned about the fine print. There are a few things that can eat away at your payments, making it feel like the interest is high. Let's talk about some of the common culprits.\n\nOne of the most common is the \"origination fee\". This is a fee charged by the lender when you first take out the loan. It's usually a percentage of the loan amount, and it's added to the principal balance. So, if you have a $10,000 loan with a 4% origination fee, you'll actually owe $10,400 when you start making payments.\n\nAnother thing to watch out for is the \"accrual interest\" or \"daily accrual interest\". This is interest that's charged on the outstanding balance, but it's not always compounded monthly. Instead, it's calculated daily and added to the principal balance. This can make the balance seem like it's growing faster than it actually is.\n\nAnd then there's the \"late payment fee\". If you miss a payment, you'll typically be charged a fee, which can range from $25 to $35. This can add up quickly, especially if you're making multiple late payments.\n\nFinally, there's the \"",
